# Ledger — {{PROJECT_NAME}}

The team's shared record of what the loop did.

```
ledger/                     a git WORKTREE on the ops branch (created by /sdlc-ledger)
├── entries/<actor>.jsonl   one file per person — you only ever write your own
└── TEAM.md                 generated view; regenerate, never hand-edit
```

**Set it up once per clone:** turn `"ledger": {"enabled": true}` on in `.sdlc/config.json`, then run
**`/sdlc-ledger`** (or `python3 <loop-scripts>/sync.py bootstrap .sdlc`) — one command creates the ops
branch, seeds your entries file + `TEAM.md`, and **pushes** so the team can see it. Then add
`.sdlc/ledger/` to `.gitignore` on your code branch. `/sdlc-doctor` runs this for you if it sees the
ledger enabled but not set up. The ledger lives on its own branch (default `sdlc-ledger`), so pulling
it never touches your code checkout — and that branch is never merged into your integration branch, so
it needs no review. `sync.py pull` / `publish` move it; `bash <loop-scripts>/watch.sh .sdlc` does both
on an interval and writes `.sdlc/state/inbox.md`, which `loop.py next` surfaces between goals.

**Why one file per person.** Concurrent appends to a shared file conflict, in the filesystem
and again in git. Owning exactly one file each removes the conflict by construction; the team
view is the union of the files, computed on read.

**Read it:**

```bash
python3 <loop-scripts>/ledger.py summary .sdlc          # counts + outstanding hand-offs
python3 <loop-scripts>/ledger.py mine    .sdlc          # what is addressed to me
python3 <loop-scripts>/ledger.py render  .sdlc --write  # regenerate TEAM.md
```

**Write it** — the loop records `claimed` and the outcome by itself once
`"ledger": {"enabled": true}` is set. Anything else is an explicit append:

```bash
python3 <loop-scripts>/ledger.py append .sdlc note 0007-cache.md --why "spike looks viable"
```

One line per entry: `id · ts · actor · kind · goal`, plus any of
`area · to · issue · priority · why · state · ref`. Kinds are
`claimed · done · parked · failed · handoff · ack · release · note`.
An entry with a `to` is addressed to that person and shows up in the team view.

**The claim lease.** A `claimed` with no later `done`/`parked`/`failed` is an
open lease: the loop won't start a goal another actor holds one on, so two people
on one board don't do the same work twice (a goal *you* hold is still yours to
resume). It's advisory — it sees claims already pulled to the ops branch, not a
distributed lock — so keep per-person assignee routing on too. A crashed
claimer's hold expires after `ledger.lease.ttl_hours` (default 12) so it can't
block the team forever.
